          "id": "SP_THLIPTODON_DIAPHANUS",
          "name": "Thliptodon diaphanus",
          "rank": "SPECIES",
          "commonName": "",
          "lineage": "Thliptodon",
          "subspeciesCount": 0,
          "description": "<p>External description: Anterior body regions of living worm are reddish purple, whereas posteriorly the trunk is slightly purple, with the beige guts visible through the very thin body wall. The worm has a proboscis shaped as a smooth dome with a collar shaped as bilateral lips. The dorsal trunk is comprised of genital wings, which look like folded sheets with the edges meeting in the dorsal midline. The trunk is divided into short prehepatic and hepatic intestinal regions and a long posthepatic intestine. A lateroventral fold runs along the prehepatic and hepatic intestinal regions, narrowing as it reaches the anus.</p><p>[Priede, 249]</p><p>Internal description: The skin covering the proboscis is comprised of support cells and mucous gland cells. The proboscis coelom and the lips both consist of a diffuse mesh of smooth muscle and connective tissue cells. The nervous system can be found along the entire specimen, but is most conspicuous mid-dorsally along the proboscis stalk.</p><p>The genital wings cover the pharyngeal region where the gill pores are located. On the concave surfaces of each genital wing, the ovaries and testes were found in even numbers. The ovoid testis consists of a peripheral zone of spermatogenic cells surrounded by a lumen of sperm cells. The ovaries each consist of a thin germ epithelium surrounding a single oocyte wiht a visible nucleus and nucleolus.",
          "sourcedFrom": "gbif",
          "wikipediaTitle": "gbif:2305790"
        },
